ype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"blind_id\":\"HOX037\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":1,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"Says an ICAV may prepare sub-fund financial statements and can elect in its constitution to dispense with AGMs. Says a plc prepares company-level statutory accounts with sub-fund sections only, and is generally subject to company-law AGM requirements. The hedges ('where permitted', 'exceptions may apply') soften the contrast but the minimum is conveyed.\"},{\"point\":2,\"credit\":0,\"reason\":\"Says only that 'Neither form automatically qualifies for US check-the-box treatment' and that classification 'depend[s] on entity eligibility'. It never says the ICAV can elect US partnership treatment. It never says the investment plc is a per-se corporation that cannot elect. The answer treats both vehicles the same, so the required contrast is missing.\"}],\"central_error\":null,\"local_error\":null,\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"Point 1 is covered and point 2 is not, giving 1 of 2 required coverage, which meets the half threshold. Core facts on accounts and AGMs are supplied. There is no explicit reversal of either vehicle's check-the-box eligibility, so no central error. The vague 'neither automatically qualifies' wording is non-committal rather than an affirmative falsehood, so it counts as omission. The ICAV entity-level accounts remark is not contradicted by the sources. Result: partial.\"}","ttft_ms":5414,"type":"result","duration_ms":9616,"uuid":"34066629-78e6-47a4-ab45-6ccd0e191892","ttft_stream_ms":538,"time_to_request_ms":58,"first_content_frame_ms":538,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
